WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:07.450
DAVIDSON: Welcome to today's Council on Foreign
Relations Power Profile meeting on Bashar

00:00:07.450 --> 00:00:08.450
al-Assad.

00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:13.360
Let me introduce our speakers.

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:17.660
Right next to me is Joshua Landis-I'll keep
the backgrounds short, since you have the

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:28.029
biographies in your-in your packets-David
Lesch, and Andrew Tabler, not Tobler.

00:00:28.029 --> 00:00:29.029
TABLER: (Laughs.)

00:00:29.029 --> 00:00:35.000
DAVIDSON: So this is, again, a Power Profile,
so I wanted to frame the discussion a little

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:41.520
bit by thinking about what that-what that
means, what the power and the profile-the

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:49.140
personality and the exercise of governing
power-mean together.

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:54.300
Profiles are something that you can have more
than one of at a given time, as Assad certainly

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:56.350
has in his career.

00:00:56.350 --> 00:01:02.820
But then there are moments when there's only
one option for the power, where you can't

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:04.650
keep so many faces.

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:13.830
So I want to maybe start with one of those
moments for thinking about Assad, and just

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:19.870
bring the three of you back to the morning
of March 30th, 2011.

00:01:19.870 --> 00:01:22.370
This is a time when Syria is on edge.

00:01:22.370 --> 00:01:26.820
Dozens of protesters-which seemed like a lot
at the time-had been killed.

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:31.740
There have been pictures of child protesters
who have been tortured.

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:35.070
There's a great deal of pressure on the regime.

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:41.520
Assad hasn't been seen in two weeks, and he's
giving a highly anticipated speech to the

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:47.500
Syrian parliament, and there's an expectation
that's hardened into an assumption that he's

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:50.590
going to announce major reforms.

00:01:50.590 --> 00:01:52.920
That doesn't happen.

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:57.040
He comes out and talks about people being
duped and conspiracies.

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:02.780
So I want-you know, David, maybe you can start
by talking about the shock of that moment

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:08.640
and its implications-what it meant, both for
this idea of who he was and the exercise of

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:09.910
power in Syria.

00:02:09.910 --> 00:02:11.470
LESCH: Yeah.

00:02:11.470 --> 00:02:17.050
Well, there was-during the course of my research,
and involved in a particular project on Syria

00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:22.980
on which I'm working over the last three years,
I had the opportunity to connect again with

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:29.730
many people who are close to Assad, some of
whom are still with him, others who are in

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:33.920
exile or defected or left the government for
one reason or another.

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:39.960
And a consistent picture emerged when examining
this, is that there was a lot of confusion,

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:46.760
and potentially intrigue in the-certainly
the weeks and the days since the disturbances

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:48.820
in Deraa and Assad's speech.

00:02:48.820 --> 00:02:53.270
And there was a lot of mystery of, you know,
where he was, you know, what was he going

00:02:53.270 --> 00:02:58.569
to do in this speech, which I think is a seminal
moment in modern Middle East history.

00:02:58.569 --> 00:03:06.140
And what I learned from many of these people
who were involved in the-in the preparation

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:10.140
for the speech is that there were many different
drafts.

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:16.020
One person, who is a close friend of Assad
who's no longer in the government, he saw

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:22.800
a draft about an hour before Bashar went to
the Syrian parliament to deliver his speech,

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:26.430
and it was full of concessions and announcements
of reforms.

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:31.420
And so, when Assad gave his speech in the
parliament on TV and this guy was watching

00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:34.180
it on TV, he was shocked to see that it was
another version.

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:40.280
And, in his mind and other people who I met
who were involved in that particular situation,

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:44.709
they believe that some of the security chiefs
got to Assad.

00:03:44.709 --> 00:03:49.540
And there has been a tug-there was a tug-of-war
between a number of different individuals,

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:56.390
some counseling, you know, that he make concessions,
some that he cracked down, and that ultimately

00:03:56.390 --> 00:04:01.300
the security chiefs convinced Assad that they
could put down the uprising in a couple of

00:04:01.300 --> 00:04:04.350
weeks, and then they could just go back to
normal.

00:04:04.350 --> 00:04:12.099
And so he gave that speech-which, you know,
reflects the fact that he's someone that a

00:04:12.099 --> 00:04:15.580
lot of people had hope for when he came into
power as a reformer.

00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:20.299
And this was-in my view, this was his moment,
you know, and a lot of people in Syria-opposition

00:04:20.299 --> 00:04:23.379
and pro-Assad-were waiting for this moment.

00:04:23.379 --> 00:04:27.550
Could he be the person they all thought he
hoped he could be, finally stand up to the

00:04:27.550 --> 00:04:29.449
hardliners and do something?

00:04:29.449 --> 00:04:33.949
And obviously, he did not, and there was a
great deal of disappointment-a lot of disappointment

00:04:33.949 --> 00:04:38.289
among Syrian officials and government people
with whom I've spoken, not just the opposition.

00:04:38.289 --> 00:04:44.259
DAVIDSON: So, in a way, each draft of that
speech represents a different profile of who

00:04:44.259 --> 00:04:45.259
Assad was.

00:04:45.259 --> 00:04:51.669
Andrew, why do you think he picked up one
draft rather than the other?

00:04:51.669 --> 00:04:56.670
TABLER: I think he saw what happened in Cairo
and had happened in Tunisia, and I think he

00:04:56.670 --> 00:04:59.300
feared that it was going to happen to him.

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:03.509
I think that would be certainly a key factor,
I think.

00:05:03.509 --> 00:05:10.960
I thought it was very interesting that the
drafts-it wasn't just that there were drafts.

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:13.439
That was well-known, and David's completely
right.

00:05:13.439 --> 00:05:18.669
It's that they were actually given, full paragraph,
to David Ignatius to write in The Washington

00:05:18.669 --> 00:05:22.449
Post as a-as a prelude to this great announcement.

00:05:22.449 --> 00:05:27.349
And then, when it completely ended up being
the reverse, I realized that we were going

00:05:27.349 --> 00:05:30.220
down a very, very different road.

00:05:30.220 --> 00:05:38.210
And I think it's-you know, one of the things
that struck me the most living in Syria, especially-and

00:05:38.210 --> 00:05:41.680
this was something that people close to the
regime used to speak with me about often-was

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:49.439
the-how much more unpredictable Bashar was
than his father, and they were adamant about

00:05:49.439 --> 00:05:50.439
that.

00:05:50.439 --> 00:05:51.540
They used the word "moody."

00:05:51.540 --> 00:05:55.460
In this case, I think he probably was on the
horns of a dilemma.

00:05:55.460 --> 00:06:00.409
He could go down the road that David had mentioned,
or he could try and shoot his way out of it.

00:06:00.409 --> 00:06:04.969
And unfortunately, he chose the latter, and
we have this result.

00:06:04.969 --> 00:06:09.569
DAVIDSON: Josh, do you want to-
LANDIS: Well, I think-I think what we've seen

00:06:09.569 --> 00:06:14.729
out of Assad is that the regime is extremely
brittle.

00:06:14.729 --> 00:06:16.990
He can't really reform.

00:06:16.990 --> 00:06:20.800
I have very little hopes that there is going
to be any reform in the regime.

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:26.490
What we've seen over five years is the regime
shrink to a very small little part of Syria,

00:06:26.490 --> 00:06:27.930
and it would have shrunk further.

00:06:27.930 --> 00:06:31.749
And it can expand, but it can't reform.

00:06:31.749 --> 00:06:38.009
The Alawites, I think, understand that if
they begin to dissemble their regime, they're

00:06:38.009 --> 00:06:39.379
going to fall apart.

00:06:39.379 --> 00:06:44.409
DAVIDSON: Well, let's just-let me just interrupt
and say I'm sure most people know, but Assad

00:06:44.409 --> 00:06:48.159
and his family are members of the Alawite
minority community.

00:06:48.159 --> 00:06:54.830
So maybe you could also just throw in a little
bit about what that means about the personalities

00:06:54.830 --> 00:06:57.930
of the regime and about Assad's own connections.

00:06:57.930 --> 00:07:03.649
LANDIS: Well, I mean, I'm sure all of you
are familiar with the Alawite sect to a certain

00:07:03.649 --> 00:07:04.800
degree.

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:09.279
It's 12 percent-10 to 12 percent of the Syrian
population.

00:07:09.279 --> 00:07:16.199
When the French arrived in 1920, the Alawites
were segregated demographically almost entirely

00:07:16.199 --> 00:07:17.199
from the Sunni community.

00:07:17.199 --> 00:07:22.590
There was no town of over 200 people in which
Sunnis and Alawites lived together.

00:07:22.590 --> 00:07:28.839
Latakia, Jableh, Tartus, Baniyas, the coastal
cities were all Sunni cities with large Christian

00:07:28.839 --> 00:07:29.839
minorities.

00:07:29.839 --> 00:07:33.389
There were, of course, Alawite servants, but
they didn't live there.

00:07:33.389 --> 00:07:39.039
The first censuses taken by the Syrians showed
that there were-they didn't have Alawites

00:07:39.039 --> 00:07:40.789
in the cities.

00:07:40.789 --> 00:07:46.259
Now, the integration that's taken place has
taken place since then.

00:07:46.259 --> 00:07:51.619
And it is-it's important, but not that important.

00:07:51.619 --> 00:07:53.050
It's modern.

00:07:53.050 --> 00:07:57.009
The Alawites, when they came to Damascus in
big numbers after Hafez al-Assad took power

00:07:57.009 --> 00:08:02.479
in the '70s, were called muwafeddin (ph),
concurrent commonly in Damascus.

00:08:02.479 --> 00:08:08.189
And I heard that word many times living there
in the 1980, '81, '82, when I was a Fulbrighter

00:08:08.189 --> 00:08:10.679
there, and at the time of Hama.

00:08:10.679 --> 00:08:15.349
These muwafeddin (ph) is people who have come
in, are alien from-who have come in from the

00:08:15.349 --> 00:08:16.439
outside.

00:08:16.439 --> 00:08:19.479
And this has been the dilemma.

00:08:19.479 --> 00:08:25.020
The Alawites were met-the Muslim Brotherhood
from the beginning, in the 1950s and '60s-Said

00:08:25.020 --> 00:08:31.530
Hawwa, the big intellectual of the Syrian
Muslim Brotherhood, described the Alawites

00:08:31.530 --> 00:08:36.529
as unbelievers, and that this was a war of
ridda, they're murtadin (ph), because they're

00:08:36.529 --> 00:08:44.279
apostates, and it would be like Abu Bakr,
the first caliph, having to reconvert Arabia

00:08:44.279 --> 00:08:48.959
to Islam after the Arab tribes pulled away
from Islam and forsook Mohammed.

00:08:48.959 --> 00:08:51.420
DAVIDSON: And yet, Assad married somebody
who is from outside that community.

00:08:51.420 --> 00:08:55.950
LANDIS: Yes, he has, and so has the Jordanian
king.

00:08:55.950 --> 00:08:59.110
And so, you know, I think this is nation-building.

00:08:59.110 --> 00:09:00.529
You know, the Saudis made love, not war.

00:09:00.529 --> 00:09:05.069
Of course, they made war first, but then they
made love, and they tried to unite their kingdom

00:09:05.069 --> 00:09:06.850
on the marriage bed.

00:09:06.850 --> 00:09:09.149
And Assad was doing the same thing.

00:09:09.149 --> 00:09:13.839
Of course, he couldn't have four wives or,
you know, many more, as the Saudi kings did,

00:09:13.839 --> 00:09:18.220
but nation-building was part of the process.

00:09:18.220 --> 00:09:21.110
But the Alawite community felt very estranged.

00:09:21.110 --> 00:09:29.860
And when they-when this first shooting began,
the security people were telling everybody

00:09:29.860 --> 00:09:35.860
that they had been rolling up two or three
cells of jihadists every month for the last

00:09:35.860 --> 00:09:42.600
several years, since the war in Iraq, and
that if you let this demonstration of people-young

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:47.819
boys who are young-the median age in Syria
is 21, so most of those people out there didn't

00:09:47.819 --> 00:09:52.810
know Hama, they didn't know the bloodshed
that had proceeded.

00:09:52.810 --> 00:09:53.860
They wanted reforms.

00:09:53.860 --> 00:10:01.380
But if you let them begin to go down that
road of losing power, that the jihadis were

00:10:01.380 --> 00:10:02.380
going to take over.

00:10:02.380 --> 00:10:04.810
I think the regime people felt sure of that.

00:10:04.810 --> 00:10:11.180
And so, when-if Assad had been tempted to
go down the road of reform, the people around

00:10:11.180 --> 00:10:16.110
him said, you're a fool, you're na�ve, you
don't understand the region.

00:10:16.110 --> 00:10:19.350
We're the ones who have been keeping these
jihadis down.

00:10:19.350 --> 00:10:20.779
We've been rolling them up.

00:10:20.779 --> 00:10:24.550
We've been controlling them, manipulating
them, sending them into Iraq.

00:10:24.550 --> 00:10:25.839
You can't do this.

00:10:25.839 --> 00:10:27.360
You will be swept aside.

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:28.680
And so he shot.

00:10:28.680 --> 00:10:32.279
And that's where we've been ever since, and
I think it will be very difficult for him

00:10:32.279 --> 00:10:38.480
to let go without fearing terrible revenge
for the community and for, of course, his

00:10:38.480 --> 00:10:39.480
family.

00:10:39.480 --> 00:10:45.449
LESCH: It's interesting, on the subject of
those surrounding the speech, I actually wrote

00:10:45.449 --> 00:10:50.560
a letter to him through a contact I had in
the-in the Syrian government, and I never

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:52.420
knew whether or not he read the letter.

00:10:52.420 --> 00:10:59.160
And it was, you know, advising him or whatever,
just making some suggestions regarding what

00:10:59.160 --> 00:11:03.060
he could do in terms of reform-presidential
term limits, some of this sort of thing-trying

00:11:03.060 --> 00:11:07.399
to encourage him to be the person that we
all thought, you know, he could be, sort of

00:11:07.399 --> 00:11:08.920
that sort of thing.

00:11:08.920 --> 00:11:13.139
And he-the interesting thing about it is I
learned, you know, a couple of years later

00:11:13.139 --> 00:11:16.019
from two different people that he did, in
fact, read it.

00:11:16.019 --> 00:11:19.819
And what's interesting about that is not so
much that he read it-and obviously rejected-(laughs)-anything

00:11:19.819 --> 00:11:25.949
I said-but that there were people from two
different sources close to him who brought

00:11:25.949 --> 00:11:31.350
it to him, who obviously read it and wanted
him, perhaps, to go down this road.

00:11:31.350 --> 00:11:40.190
I agree with my friends Andrew and Josh that,
you know, I always thought that Assad-again,

00:11:40.190 --> 00:11:41.769
many people hoped he would change the system.

00:11:41.769 --> 00:11:47.199
It seemed that the system changed him, and
that this system has a reflexive, convulsive

00:11:47.199 --> 00:11:51.160
reaction to disturbance; and they use the
hammer, they don't use the velvet glove.

00:11:51.160 --> 00:11:54.660
DAVIDSON: Well, this is an interesting question,
because it gets to the matter of agency.

00:11:54.660 --> 00:12:00.819
You know, I went back and looked at the coverage
of this speed, and the Times report included

00:12:00.819 --> 00:12:06.980
this line: "What the speech did accomplish"-it
had mentioned all the things it didn't accomplish,

00:12:06.980 --> 00:12:11.950
which were many-"was to explode a narrative
that had been written about the president

00:12:11.950 --> 00:12:17.800
since he took office 11 years ago, that his
efforts at reform were being blocked by holdovers

00:12:17.800 --> 00:12:19.829
from the era of his father, Hafez al-Assad."

00:12:19.829 --> 00:12:26.240
But in a way, what you're saying is that it
didn't so much explode that narrative, but

00:12:26.240 --> 00:12:31.279
say "and they've won"-"and the holdovers have
won."

00:12:31.279 --> 00:12:36.449
It brings us to the question, did Assad at
that moment have power?

00:12:36.449 --> 00:12:40.220
Did-
LESCH: The holdovers were gone.

00:12:40.220 --> 00:12:45.110
By 2005, you know, the Baath Party Regional
Congress in that summer, I mean, he was putting

00:12:45.110 --> 00:12:46.110
his people in.

00:12:46.110 --> 00:12:51.410
Josh said it very well after the Hariri assassination
and withdrawal from Lebanon, that he may have

00:12:51.410 --> 00:12:53.779
lost Beirut but he gained Damascus.

00:12:53.779 --> 00:12:58.759
And so he used that episode to really secure
his way and power.

00:12:58.759 --> 00:13:04.759
And when I, you know, visited him in 2005
and early 2006, his people were in the Cabinet,

00:13:04.759 --> 00:13:08.779
they were in the Baath Party regional command,
in the military security apparatus.

00:13:08.779 --> 00:13:14.470
So, you know, there is no excuses in terms
of, you know, him being forced to.

00:13:14.470 --> 00:13:16.810
This was his decision, and he had power.

00:13:16.810 --> 00:13:18.889
And he could have gone one way.

00:13:18.889 --> 00:13:24.180
It's easy for us armchair historians or political
scientists to say, you should have done this.

00:13:24.180 --> 00:13:26.759
You should have made the reforms and fought
against the system.

00:13:26.759 --> 00:13:31.699
I had a top Hezbollah guy tell me-who's pro-Assad-say
that he had the people with him; he could

00:13:31.699 --> 00:13:33.490
have used the people against those hardliners.

00:13:33.490 --> 00:13:37.100
DAVIDSON: So he could have pushed back against
this message that you'll be swept aside if

00:13:37.100 --> 00:13:38.579
you-
LESCH: He could have, but, you know, there

00:13:38.579 --> 00:13:41.270
was a gun to his head perhaps.

00:13:41.270 --> 00:13:45.910
DAVIDSON: Or he was-or was he-I guess the
question is whether he was holding the gun

00:13:45.910 --> 00:13:48.370
or if the gun was-(laughter).

00:13:48.370 --> 00:13:53.149
But the whole idea, the idea-the idea of this
young aspiring reformer with the fashionable

00:13:53.149 --> 00:13:56.370
British-born wife, was this always a delusion?

00:13:56.370 --> 00:13:58.170
Was it our delusion?

00:13:58.170 --> 00:14:01.449
Was it shared by Syrians as well?

00:14:01.449 --> 00:14:07.699
Was it ever real, or were we always taking
the style and not really looking at the substance,

00:14:07.699 --> 00:14:08.699
Andrew?

00:14:08.699 --> 00:14:11.410
TABLER: I think that a lot of Syrian certainly
believe it.

00:14:11.410 --> 00:14:20.120
And early on it was easier to believe because,
of course, Hafez's term had just-or he had

00:14:20.120 --> 00:14:21.120
just died.

00:14:21.120 --> 00:14:22.959
And the only way was up, really.

00:14:22.959 --> 00:14:25.319
I mean, they had survived.

00:14:25.319 --> 00:14:28.540
I think that Bashar promised a lot.

00:14:28.540 --> 00:14:32.930
And there were a lot of what became known
as, you know, the Damascus Spring and then

00:14:32.930 --> 00:14:34.899
later the reforms.

00:14:34.899 --> 00:14:37.100
There were a lot of reforms that were launched.

00:14:37.100 --> 00:14:41.120
And it was into that that I come to Syria
and saw firsthand.

00:14:41.120 --> 00:14:47.190
And people were giddy with the idea of reform,
because what had happened in the previous

00:14:47.190 --> 00:14:51.050
decades, not just under the Assads but the
political turmoil since independence until

00:14:51.050 --> 00:14:56.970
Assad, they really wanted to have some sort
of soft landing to this.

00:14:56.970 --> 00:14:59.389
But unfortunately that wasn't - that didn't
prove to be the case.

00:14:59.389 --> 00:15:04.329
DAVIDSON: Just to throw in a reminder that
Assad was only, I believe, 34 when he came

00:15:04.329 --> 00:15:08.939
to power, which is ten years younger than
Marco Rubio, for perspective.

00:15:08.939 --> 00:15:09.939
(Laughs.)

00:15:09.939 --> 00:15:13.519
So and-
LESCH: And they had to change the constitution

00:15:13.519 --> 00:15:16.060
to get-because this was-
TABLER: Yeah, to 34.

00:15:16.060 --> 00:15:17.060
(Laughter.)

00:15:17.060 --> 00:15:19.189
DAVIDSON: And you know-
LANDIS: I don't think there's a lot of contradiction

00:15:19.189 --> 00:15:23.640
between those things, in the sense that I
think Assad came to power, he was fairly na�ve,

00:15:23.640 --> 00:15:27.580
he let most of the people out of prison that
his father had imprisoned for decades, the

00:15:27.580 --> 00:15:29.110
Muslim Brothers and so forth.

00:15:29.110 --> 00:15:31.199
The prison numbers got down quite low.

00:15:31.199 --> 00:15:32.699
He thought he could modernize.

00:15:32.699 --> 00:15:40.800
Without changing politics, he thought he could
put a chicken in everybody's pot.

00:15:40.800 --> 00:15:45.059
But the reality of power remained the same.

00:15:45.059 --> 00:15:49.410
He may not have understood that he was going
to have to do that, or that he was going to

00:15:49.410 --> 00:15:54.660
have to-you know, he was going to face another
Hama and, in a sense, come out with guns blazing.

00:15:54.660 --> 00:15:55.660
But he learnt that.

00:15:55.660 --> 00:16:00.529
And that's what he's learnt over the five
years, I think, is that he has got to win

00:16:00.529 --> 00:16:03.100
this war, or else he's going to lose.

00:16:03.100 --> 00:16:04.760
And that's what he's done.

00:16:04.760 --> 00:16:08.189
He probably didn't realize that he'd ever
have to do this.

00:16:08.189 --> 00:16:16.230
And I think the awkwardness of those speeches
that he gave was of seeing a boyish guy who

00:16:16.230 --> 00:16:21.170
was na�ve trying to deny that he was going
to shoot everybody.

00:16:21.170 --> 00:16:25.370
And he finally learnt, slowly, that that's
what was going to happen.

00:16:25.370 --> 00:16:29.379
LESCH: I think, you know, one of the things,
you know, talking about when he came to power

00:16:29.379 --> 00:16:34.579
and the expectations, the expectations in
the West were way too high for him.

00:16:34.579 --> 00:16:38.700
The first time I met with him, and after we
exchanged pleasantries and I explained what

00:16:38.700 --> 00:16:42.250
I wanted to do and all that stuff, I told
him, you know, Mr. President, the biggest

00:16:42.250 --> 00:16:48.540
mistake you ever made upon coming to power
in 2000 was letting it be known that you liked

00:16:48.540 --> 00:16:51.600
Phil Collins music, the British rocker.

00:16:51.600 --> 00:16:52.630
(Laughter.)

00:16:52.630 --> 00:16:58.819
And he looked at me kind of weird, but I said,
no, because that information was disseminated.

00:16:58.819 --> 00:17:03.009
And it reinforced this image, this mythology
that was building in the West that because

00:17:03.009 --> 00:17:07.100
he was an ophthalmologist, non-traditional
path to power, because he spent 18 months

00:17:07.100 --> 00:17:11.790
in London studying an advanced degree in ophthalmology-
DAVIDSON: His father hadn't trained him to

00:17:11.790 --> 00:17:14.550
be a dictator, the way his older brother had
been trained?

00:17:14.550 --> 00:17:15.550
TABLER: There was some training.

00:17:15.550 --> 00:17:16.550
LESCH: Yeah, there was some.

00:17:16.550 --> 00:17:18.860
I mean, he had several years after Bassel
had died.

00:17:18.860 --> 00:17:21.350
And he was being groomed, although they deny
it.

00:17:21.350 --> 00:17:25.089
But there were high expectations because,
you know, he was supposed to be this modernizing

00:17:25.089 --> 00:17:29.260
reformer who would make peace with Israel
and with the United States because of this

00:17:29.260 --> 00:17:30.600
unusual background.

00:17:30.600 --> 00:17:35.130
But what people didn't understand, and what
a lot of us have been saying for years, is

00:17:35.130 --> 00:17:38.300
that, you know, he's a child of the Arab-Israeli
conflict.

00:17:38.300 --> 00:17:40.260
He's a child of a superpower of Cold War.

00:17:40.260 --> 00:17:42.110
He's a child of the tumult in Lebanon.

00:17:42.110 --> 00:17:44.890
And most of all, he's a child of Hafez al-Assad.

00:17:44.890 --> 00:17:50.550
That's what shaped his world view more than
18 months in London, Phil Collins music, and

00:17:50.550 --> 00:17:54.050
the technological toys of the West-as he's
a computer nerd and avid photographer.

00:17:54.050 --> 00:17:59.820
So there was this image that, you know, totally
was inconsistent with reality, and the reality

00:17:59.820 --> 00:18:01.480
of the Syrian system.

00:18:01.480 --> 00:18:05.930
So when these expectations were not met, certainly
in the West, therefore the disappointment

00:18:05.930 --> 00:18:06.930
was that much greater.

00:18:06.930 --> 00:18:12.720
TABLER: What Josh mentioned about how brittle
the regime was, I remember the thing that

00:18:12.720 --> 00:18:19.140
struck me, and when I realized really how
rigid it was-so when I-for a time I worked

00:18:19.140 --> 00:18:23.750
for one of Asma Assad's charities dealing
with world development.

00:18:23.750 --> 00:18:29.270
And that charity, which actually did a lot
of good work, did a lot of research in rural

00:18:29.270 --> 00:18:35.750
areas and did a lot of the-it had mapped with
the U.N. a lot of the demographic rural explosions

00:18:35.750 --> 00:18:40.510
that had happened in the 10 years after the
Hama massacre, when everybody stayed home

00:18:40.510 --> 00:18:43.110
and there was this huge spike in birthrates.

00:18:43.110 --> 00:18:49.200
And so the NGO that she had created was putting
out all of this information, including to

00:18:49.200 --> 00:18:51.540
the palace, about here is this problem.

00:18:51.540 --> 00:18:56.280
Here is this problem we're going to have to
deal with.

00:18:56.280 --> 00:19:01.580
And you're essentially sharing a bed with
the president and still, despite all the advice

00:19:01.580 --> 00:19:09.420
that would come out of that NGO, just in that
narrow sense, there were-the resulting reforms

00:19:09.420 --> 00:19:11.550
did not accommodate that growing population.

00:19:11.550 --> 00:19:18.390
So in a way he had a Sunni wife who was switched
on enough to be able to map this kind of stuff,

00:19:18.390 --> 00:19:23.260
gave that advice to the presidency, but still
at the same time the system couldn't accommodate

00:19:23.260 --> 00:19:24.260
it.

00:19:24.260 --> 00:19:25.700
It simply couldn't change.

00:19:25.700 --> 00:19:30.880
And then eventually, of course, those people
overwhelmed the system when they came out

00:19:30.880 --> 00:19:31.940
on the streets in 2011.

00:19:31.940 --> 00:19:37.900
DAVIDSON: For five years after the speech,
somewhere between a quarter of a million and

00:19:37.900 --> 00:19:40.410
a half million people are dead.

00:19:40.410 --> 00:19:42.810
Four million people in Syria are refugees.

00:19:42.810 --> 00:19:46.500
It almost-you know, we've talked a lot.

00:19:46.500 --> 00:19:54.620
Give us a sense of what we know about Assad
explains just the brutality of the war-the

00:19:54.620 --> 00:20:00.800
barrel bombs, the cities that are made to
starve, the indifference to civilian life-that

00:20:00.800 --> 00:20:04.590
the way the war has been waged, not just its
persistence.

00:20:04.590 --> 00:20:07.090
LANDIS: If I could take a stab at that.

00:20:07.090 --> 00:20:13.270
I've tried to compare what's going on in the
larger Middle East, especially the Levant,

00:20:13.270 --> 00:20:15.950
to what happened in World War II in Central
Europe.

00:20:15.950 --> 00:20:21.110
All these nations, Poland to Palestine, are
the class of 1919 Paris peace conference X

00:20:21.110 --> 00:20:24.700
multiethnic, multi-religious empires.

00:20:24.700 --> 00:20:28.750
Lines were drawn, people who didn't want to
live together were stuck together.

00:20:28.750 --> 00:20:33.800
In World War II, that explosion took place
and the borders weren't changed, but the people

00:20:33.800 --> 00:20:35.400
were changed to fit the borders.

00:20:35.400 --> 00:20:40.520
Poland was 64 percent Polish, 100 percent
Polish by the end of the war.

00:20:40.520 --> 00:20:43.550
Czechoslovakia, 32 percent minorities, all
of them killed or ethnically cleansed by the

00:20:43.550 --> 00:20:44.550
end of the war.

00:20:44.550 --> 00:20:46.370
That was true right down through Romania.

00:20:46.370 --> 00:20:51.470
Twelve million Germans ethnically cleansed
from ethnic Europe, 6 million Jews killed,

00:20:51.470 --> 00:20:52.470
Ruthenians, others.

00:20:52.470 --> 00:20:58.270
Of course, Yugoslavia came later, 1990 exploded,
six, seven nation-states made out of it.

00:20:58.270 --> 00:21:02.910
You could argue that Ukraine is being sorted
out today.

00:21:02.910 --> 00:21:05.250
This is the great sorting out, as I've called
it.

00:21:05.250 --> 00:21:08.760
The Middle East is going through a great sorting
out.

00:21:08.760 --> 00:21:13.210
We've already seen that with the Jews being
booted out of every city in the Middle East,

00:21:13.210 --> 00:21:14.210
and in Europe.

00:21:14.210 --> 00:21:20.160
They collected in Palestine, a minority, they're
the only minority that was able to, in a sense,

00:21:20.160 --> 00:21:22.270
become a majority in Palestine.

00:21:22.270 --> 00:21:26.080
But all the other states were minority states
because of the colonial experience.

00:21:26.080 --> 00:21:32.160
The Maronites in Lebanon, Sunnis in Iraq,
Alawites in Syria.

00:21:32.160 --> 00:21:39.010
And those minorities are clinging on for dear
life, because they see it as a zero-sum game.

00:21:39.010 --> 00:21:45.210
Twenty percent Christians in Anatolia, all
ethnically cleansed during the Turkish revolution.

00:21:45.210 --> 00:21:50.920
So almost all the minorities-the Christian
minorities and so forth are gone from Iraq

00:21:50.920 --> 00:21:52.860
as a result of this sorting out.

00:21:52.860 --> 00:21:55.350
And the Sunnis and Shiites fighting it out.

00:21:55.350 --> 00:21:57.020
Now, Syria's the same way.

00:21:57.020 --> 00:21:58.540
The Alawites look at this world.

00:21:58.540 --> 00:22:00.190
They say, there's no more Christians in Turkey.

00:22:00.190 --> 00:22:04.830
There are no more Palestinians in Palestine-of
course, there are, but there's not going to

00:22:04.830 --> 00:22:07.200
be a two- state solution probably.

00:22:07.200 --> 00:22:09.100
The Maronites lost.

00:22:09.100 --> 00:22:11.650
The Ba'athists lost in Iraq.

00:22:11.650 --> 00:22:14.890
They look at a very grim future.

00:22:14.890 --> 00:22:20.210
If they lost, high chances they would be ethnically
cleansed.

00:22:20.210 --> 00:22:21.780
So they barrel bombed.

00:22:21.780 --> 00:22:27.300
They would use any methods in order to destroy
their enemy.

00:22:27.300 --> 00:22:28.840
And they have.

00:22:28.840 --> 00:22:36.870
And that's because they see it as a quintessentially,
you know, existential fight.

00:22:36.870 --> 00:22:40.710
And so you know, the numbers haven't arrived
to Eastern European numbers.

00:22:40.710 --> 00:22:45.410
And of course, it's religion that defines,
in a sense, nationalism in the Middle East,

00:22:45.410 --> 00:22:47.450
not ethnicity as it was in Central Europe.

00:22:47.450 --> 00:22:48.870
So it's quite different.

00:22:48.870 --> 00:22:52.910
It's Alawites, Jews, Sunnis, Shiites, so forth,
Maronites.

00:22:52.910 --> 00:22:56.270
But what we're seeing-you know, how do you
put that back together?

00:22:56.270 --> 00:23:00.270
That's a problem, is now do you-you know,
America wants to put all those people back

00:23:00.270 --> 00:23:01.820
in power-sharing units.

00:23:01.820 --> 00:23:07.640
They're trying to destroy this giant Sunni
state that's been created by ISIS, and to

00:23:07.640 --> 00:23:15.170
put everybody back into these 1919 states
and make Shiites and Sunnis and everybody

00:23:15.170 --> 00:23:16.170
get along.

00:23:16.170 --> 00:23:22.050
And I fear that it's going to be very difficult
to do that.

00:23:22.050 --> 00:23:28.090
DAVIDSON: What role, though, did Assad play,
do you think, in making this more of a sectarian

00:23:28.090 --> 00:23:34.070
and less of political conflict, which it had
some hints of-maybe it never was one, but

00:23:34.070 --> 00:23:39.390
in himself becoming more Alawite than he'd
ever been, of other sides seeing it more in

00:23:39.390 --> 00:23:41.160
religious terms?

00:23:41.160 --> 00:23:42.230
What role did he play in that?

00:23:42.230 --> 00:23:43.280
What there a deliberation?

00:23:43.280 --> 00:23:48.600
What there an attempt to appeal to those senses?

00:23:48.600 --> 00:23:57.480
LESCH: Well, you know, it's much more nuanced
than, you know, the Alawite versus the jihadist

00:23:57.480 --> 00:23:59.040
Sunnis and so forth.

00:23:59.040 --> 00:24:06.750
You know, the Alawite leadership in Syria,
they've, you know, cultivated their ties with

00:24:06.750 --> 00:24:10.300
the Sunni business class, mercantile class.

00:24:10.300 --> 00:24:17.610
Bashar did a particularly-played a particularly
active role in cultivating Sufi orders in

00:24:17.610 --> 00:24:21.150
Syria, largely Sunni.

00:24:21.150 --> 00:24:27.340
And so it's much more mixed, his power base,
than most people figure.

00:24:27.340 --> 00:24:32.390
And in fact, many Alawites were alienated,
you know, from their fellow Alawites who were

00:24:32.390 --> 00:24:33.390
in power.

00:24:33.390 --> 00:24:38.800
Now they are, you know, coming together, congealing,
because there's really no other alternative.

00:24:38.800 --> 00:24:45.820
And that's kind of the binary situation that
Assad and his supporters have been trying

00:24:45.820 --> 00:24:50.350
to create in Syria since the beginning of
this crisis, that he is the only alternative.

00:24:50.350 --> 00:24:53.420
He's the least-worst, you know, alternative.

00:24:53.420 --> 00:24:58.610
And the other-the only other answer to this,
the only other alternative is this jihadist

00:24:58.610 --> 00:25:00.610
state of ISIS and al-Nusra.

00:25:00.610 --> 00:25:02.590
And that's unacceptable to most Syrians.

00:25:02.590 --> 00:25:08.770
TABLER: I think one way that he's made it
sectarian is that-and David's right.

00:25:08.770 --> 00:25:09.770
It is complicated.

00:25:09.770 --> 00:25:13.800
It's not like all minorities back Assad and
all Sunnis back-the Sunni community's extremely

00:25:13.800 --> 00:25:14.800
diverse.

00:25:14.800 --> 00:25:18.560
But the one way that he has made it, I think,
sectarian is that-so the biggest problem in

00:25:18.560 --> 00:25:23.790
Syria for the regime is reliable manpower.

00:25:23.790 --> 00:25:30.780
And because of the president's response to
the uprising, recruiting that reliable manpower

00:25:30.780 --> 00:25:33.780
from the majority Sunni population is difficult.

00:25:33.780 --> 00:25:39.740
So what he did, and what has subsequently
happened, is that they reorganized the Syrian

00:25:39.740 --> 00:25:40.740
forces.

00:25:40.740 --> 00:25:42.680
It's not just the Syrian-Arab Army anymore.

00:25:42.680 --> 00:25:46.880
But you have the national defense forces,
which were partially trained up and organized

00:25:46.880 --> 00:25:53.740
by the IRGC Quds Force from Iran, but also
invited in Hezbollah and Shia militias from

00:25:53.740 --> 00:25:57.970
Iraq, Pakistan and Afghanistan.

00:25:57.970 --> 00:26:00.460
And that's not new.

00:26:00.460 --> 00:26:04.970
What is-what causes problems is when they
start showing up in places-when these Shia

00:26:04.970 --> 00:26:10.340
forces start showing up in places where there
aren't a lot of Shia, like in southern Syria,

00:26:10.340 --> 00:26:12.640
for example, the Shaykh Maskin battle.

00:26:12.640 --> 00:26:19.100
And so with that, it, you know, unfortunately
plays into the jihadist narrative that there's

00:26:19.100 --> 00:26:25.340
this, you know, Iranian-backed alliance supporting
Assad, which is true, but then they take it

00:26:25.340 --> 00:26:29.100
a step further and say, no, actually the United
States is in league with it.

00:26:29.100 --> 00:26:32.490
And then that causes all-you know, and they
use it in these different ways that end up,

00:26:32.490 --> 00:26:34.620
you know, costing Americans their lives.

00:26:34.620 --> 00:26:40.610
DAVIDSON: Talking about power, power means
you can do something-or that's one meaning

00:26:40.610 --> 00:26:41.940
of it.

00:26:41.940 --> 00:26:44.660
Could Assad end this?

00:26:44.660 --> 00:26:50.990
Could he-could he do something that would
end this in some way, even theoretically?

00:26:50.990 --> 00:26:54.290
And what would that look like?

00:26:54.290 --> 00:26:58.140
You're laughing, so you get to start.

00:26:58.140 --> 00:27:00.240
LESCH: He could walk out the door.

00:27:00.240 --> 00:27:01.240
That would end it.

00:27:01.240 --> 00:27:02.240
(Laughs.)

00:27:02.240 --> 00:27:03.240
But he's not going to do that.

00:27:03.240 --> 00:27:04.550
DAVIDSON: Would that do it?

00:27:04.550 --> 00:27:05.550
Would that do it?

00:27:05.550 --> 00:27:06.550
You know, we've talked a lot about the structures
and-

00:27:06.550 --> 00:27:07.880
LESCH: Well, let's put it this way, he's not
going to do it.

00:27:07.880 --> 00:27:15.070
You know, I was in Brussels-Lela (sp) was
there with me-about a month and a half ago.

00:27:15.070 --> 00:27:20.570
And there was a group of NGOs working on this
situation.

00:27:20.570 --> 00:27:26.650
And, you know, talking about-and we were informing
Staffan de Mistura, you know, making recommendations

00:27:26.650 --> 00:27:29.400
at the time the negotiations were going on.

00:27:29.400 --> 00:27:34.620
And I just made this impassioned speech, if
you will, that there's just no way-I know

00:27:34.620 --> 00:27:36.470
these guys-there's no way they're going to
give up power.

00:27:36.470 --> 00:27:37.950
There's just no way.

00:27:37.950 --> 00:27:39.700
And they will fight.

00:27:39.700 --> 00:27:44.600
They will-they think-and Assad-and I saw this
even before the uprising, that the well-being

00:27:44.600 --> 00:27:46.320
of the country is synonymous with his well-being.

00:27:46.320 --> 00:27:52.260
You know, this bubble, this alternative reality
that Roger Owen talked about, the preeminent

00:27:52.260 --> 00:27:54.850
Middle East scholar in his book "Presidents
for Life."

00:27:54.850 --> 00:27:58.270
You know, this alternate reality is constructed
around these authoritarian leaders.

00:27:58.270 --> 00:28:01.440
And they see the world in a different conceptual
paradigm.

00:28:01.440 --> 00:28:04.200
And the nature of threat is different.

00:28:04.200 --> 00:28:09.200
And so-and they define that threat and what
to do about it in a very different way than

00:28:09.200 --> 00:28:12.000
those people outside of this bubble, even
in Syria.

00:28:12.000 --> 00:28:15.660
LANDIS: I think that he thinks that he's going
to reconquer the country.

00:28:15.660 --> 00:28:16.940
Russia's going to help him.

00:28:16.940 --> 00:28:18.540
And that will make everything right.

00:28:18.540 --> 00:28:24.850
And that the foreigner, jihadist conspirators
will all be kicked out, and that Syria will

00:28:24.850 --> 00:28:25.850
be saved.

00:28:25.850 --> 00:28:26.950
And that's his slogan.

00:28:26.950 --> 00:28:30.500
That's what he believes and that's what he's
intending to do.

00:28:30.500 --> 00:28:32.770
And I think he believes it.

00:28:32.770 --> 00:28:34.020
LESCH: Agree-absolutely agree.

00:28:34.020 --> 00:28:39.320
DAVIDSON: But speaking about leaders in a
bubble, let me ask you just-we're about to

00:28:39.320 --> 00:28:45.170
open up to member's questions-so let me quickly
ask you just a lightning round thing.

00:28:45.170 --> 00:28:52.470
Next year you're advising President Trump
on dealing-(laughter)-dealing with Assad.

00:28:52.470 --> 00:28:55.830
You're not going to get him to read a briefing
book.

00:28:55.830 --> 00:29:03.220
So what's the one phrase you want him to keep
in mind when he's dealing directly with Assad

00:29:03.220 --> 00:29:09.400
in the way that he wants voters to keep low
energy in mind when they're dealing with looking

00:29:09.400 --> 00:29:11.310
at Jeb Bush or "lyin' Ted."

00:29:11.310 --> 00:29:17.340
What's that slogan you want him to have in
his head when he's dealing with that name,

00:29:17.340 --> 00:29:22.730
nickname, that one little phrase that sums-that
would be helpful for President Trump.

00:29:22.730 --> 00:29:24.590
TABLER: You mean he would be directly engaging
with Assad?

00:29:24.590 --> 00:29:29.400
DAVIDSON: Engaging maybe not in a room, but
in dealing in-yeah, yes.

00:29:29.400 --> 00:29:35.240
TABLER: I don't think any American president
will be directly engaging with Assad-

00:29:35.240 --> 00:29:36.240
DAVIDSON: Or indirectly.

00:29:36.240 --> 00:29:37.240
TABLER: Or indirectly.

00:29:37.240 --> 00:29:39.590
But you're saying how would you essentially
deal with this?

00:29:39.590 --> 00:29:41.270
DAVIDSON: What would you tell him?

00:29:41.270 --> 00:29:44.970
When he's, like, Jeb was low energy, he's
"lyin' Ted," who is this guy?

00:29:44.970 --> 00:29:46.080
What's useful for him?

00:29:46.080 --> 00:29:51.080
TABLER: Yeah, I mean, the fact is, it gets
back to your original setup, right, like which

00:29:51.080 --> 00:29:52.610
guy is this?

00:29:52.610 --> 00:29:53.610
How do we figure out?

00:29:53.610 --> 00:29:54.611
You know, how do you figure out?

00:29:54.611 --> 00:29:59.150
Is it Bashar, the reasonable reformer or the
hardline dictator who's killed all these people?

00:29:59.150 --> 00:30:03.140
Well, the only way to cut through that, that
I know of, if you would have to put it into

00:30:03.140 --> 00:30:04.800
hard dilemmas.

00:30:04.800 --> 00:30:09.860
And the only way you can put him into hard
dilemmas is you'd have to be willing to follow

00:30:09.860 --> 00:30:12.200
through on whatever dilemma you put him into.

00:30:12.200 --> 00:30:15.430
DAVIDSON: This is President Trump, so you
just have to give him one-sentence advice.

00:30:15.430 --> 00:30:16.430
(Laughter.)

00:30:16.430 --> 00:30:24.710
I'm not sure there is a really easy one sentence
to-I would say set up your dilemmas well.

00:30:24.710 --> 00:30:25.710
LESCH: OK.

00:30:25.710 --> 00:30:33.160
I would say-if he was meeting with Assad,
and I am President Trump right now-(laughter)-which

00:30:33.160 --> 00:30:39.520
is the first and only time this will happen-I'd
say: Mr. President, you have to give up enough

00:30:39.520 --> 00:30:40.520
power.

00:30:40.520 --> 00:30:43.680
The crux of any sort of political settlement
is: You have to give up enough power, if you're

00:30:43.680 --> 00:30:48.710
still in power and remain in power for the
foreseeable future, to satisfy the minimum

00:30:48.710 --> 00:30:53.760
demands of a critical mass of the opposition
of the rest of the Syrian population that

00:30:53.760 --> 00:30:59.530
has moved on, that has gone on with their
lives, and have been empowered with five years

00:30:59.530 --> 00:31:00.530
without the state.

00:31:00.530 --> 00:31:02.210
DAVIDSON: So he's the guy who's got to give
up some power?

00:31:02.210 --> 00:31:03.210
LESCH: Yeah.

00:31:03.210 --> 00:31:04.210
It ain't going to happen, but.

00:31:04.210 --> 00:31:05.210
(Laughs.)

00:31:05.210 --> 00:31:07.930
LANDIS: I would say, it's a bad deal.

00:31:07.930 --> 00:31:09.920
(Laughter.)

00:31:09.920 --> 00:31:10.950
That's what I would say.

00:31:10.950 --> 00:31:11.950
It's a bad deal.

00:31:11.950 --> 00:31:14.040
He said it himself many times.

00:31:14.040 --> 00:31:17.060
This part of the Middle East is a bad deal.

00:31:17.060 --> 00:31:20.830
We can spend trillions of dollars-
DAVIDSON: So your advice is stay away from

00:31:20.830 --> 00:31:21.830
there?

00:31:21.830 --> 00:31:22.830
LANDIS: Get out of there.

00:31:22.830 --> 00:31:23.830
DAVIDSON: The guy to stay away from.

00:31:23.830 --> 00:31:27.930
LANDIS: There are more Shiites from one end
of Lebanon to the other end of Iraq than there

00:31:27.930 --> 00:31:28.960
are Sunni Arabs.

00:31:28.960 --> 00:31:33.210
There is a majority Shiites from one end of
that region to the other.

00:31:33.210 --> 00:31:38.710
Iran and Russia are committed to a Shiite
crescent, if you will, a new security zone

00:31:38.710 --> 00:31:40.150
that's there.

00:31:40.150 --> 00:31:41.530
We can fight them for it.

00:31:41.530 --> 00:31:45.700
And I've argued with many people about-you
know, there are people in Washington, lots,

00:31:45.700 --> 00:31:49.190
who want to fight Russia for that security
belt.

00:31:49.190 --> 00:31:53.380
DAVIDSON: All right, and I bet there are people
who have-who have great questions.

00:31:53.380 --> 00:31:54.380
How about you, right there?

00:31:54.380 --> 00:31:58.010
Oh, and identify yourself if you would, yeah.

00:31:58.010 --> 00:31:59.480
Q: Hi.

00:31:59.480 --> 00:32:02.430
Claire Casey, Garten Rothkopf.

00:32:02.430 --> 00:32:07.520
So much of this conversation-no today, but
just generally-is about should Assad leave,

00:32:07.520 --> 00:32:08.520
will he leave?

00:32:08.520 --> 00:32:09.520
Is that irrelevant?

00:32:09.520 --> 00:32:13.020
What I'm hearing you say is that the institutions
themselves and the regime without Assad is

00:32:13.020 --> 00:32:14.120
still unworkable.

00:32:14.120 --> 00:32:17.370
DAVIDSON: That's a great question, because
it has been the tension running through what

00:32:17.370 --> 00:32:18.370
you've said.

00:32:18.370 --> 00:32:19.370
LESCH: Yeah.

00:32:19.370 --> 00:32:20.370
LANDIS: I don't-I don't think it is.

00:32:20.370 --> 00:32:23.610
I think that many of these Middle Eastern
states are too brittle.

00:32:23.610 --> 00:32:27.430
You take out the leading families, like taking
out the Saudi family from Saudi Arabia, what

00:32:27.430 --> 00:32:28.570
would you have left?

00:32:28.570 --> 00:32:29.570
You would have nothing.

00:32:29.570 --> 00:32:31.650
You'd have a bunch of tribes fighting each
other.

00:32:31.650 --> 00:32:35.150
Probably in Jordan if you got rid of the Hashemite
family the place would crumble.

00:32:35.150 --> 00:32:38.810
And I think in Syria if you get rid of the
Assad family, the Alawite generals around

00:32:38.810 --> 00:32:43.370
him will fight each other, just the same what
the Sunni rebels have all fought each other,

00:32:43.370 --> 00:32:45.920
because there is not a democratic tradition.

00:32:45.920 --> 00:32:47.300
They all want power.

00:32:47.300 --> 00:32:51.150
They've been played that way by Assad to keep
them balanced so that they will have to fight

00:32:51.150 --> 00:32:54.490
each other in order to consolidate a new regime.

00:32:54.490 --> 00:32:58.810
So if Russia were to pluck him out with his
brother and all of his attendants, and his

00:32:58.810 --> 00:33:03.690
loyal people, you would get a hole in the
middle of that regime that would cause chaos,

00:33:03.690 --> 00:33:05.250
coups, instability.

00:33:05.250 --> 00:33:12.650
And Russia won't do it, because Assad has
rigged-he has made the state into a-in a sense,

00:33:12.650 --> 00:33:18.960
a reflection of himself, with Alawites stacked
and his family at the core, because he knows

00:33:18.960 --> 00:33:22.170
that a lot of people are trying to do just
what we're talking about.

00:33:22.170 --> 00:33:24.700
And America's been trying to do it for decades.

00:33:24.700 --> 00:33:27.880
And he needed to coup-proof that regime.

00:33:27.880 --> 00:33:32.200
And he's made it into a mine, so that it will
turn out to be an Afghanistan if they get

00:33:32.200 --> 00:33:33.200
rid of him.

00:33:33.200 --> 00:33:36.290
LESCH: And indeed, that's Russia's-one of
their main arguments is, you know, what has

00:33:36.290 --> 00:33:41.000
regime change done in the Middle East with
the U.S., and particularly in Iraq, and what

00:33:41.000 --> 00:33:42.000
happened in Libya?

00:33:42.000 --> 00:33:44.170
You've had the collapse of the state.

00:33:44.170 --> 00:33:48.660
So regardless whether or not you like Assad,
this is keeping at least what's left together

00:33:48.660 --> 00:33:54.360
and any hope of something that resembles Syria
staying together.

00:33:54.360 --> 00:33:57.170
TABLER: The problem, though, is that it's
not stable.

00:33:57.170 --> 00:34:02.790
I mean, if this is stability-I mean, I realize
it could get more chaotic, but the sectarian

00:34:02.790 --> 00:34:08.061
war that's generated, you know, and actually,
you know, attracts foreign fighters, the number

00:34:08.061 --> 00:34:13.240
of migrants and the-you know, the cleaning
Josh was talking about, those kinds of things

00:34:13.240 --> 00:34:16.320
are unhinging European security.

00:34:16.320 --> 00:34:22.520
Unfortunately, Assad staying is going to mean
instability to a very high degree.

00:34:22.520 --> 00:34:26.070
And the question is whether we can live with
that or not.

00:34:26.070 --> 00:34:29.629
Q: Good afternoon.

00:34:29.629 --> 00:34:35.550
Ariel Cohen, the Atlantic Council.

00:34:35.550 --> 00:34:42.440
Looking at the instabilities that you all
described in Syria, Iraq, et cetera, is it

00:34:42.440 --> 00:34:51.690
in our interest to sort of facilitate and
develop the ostensible alliance with the Sunnis

00:34:51.690 --> 00:34:58.200
and draw Russia and Iran in a protracted conflict
there, because we don't want them to dominate

00:34:58.200 --> 00:34:59.230
that area?

00:34:59.230 --> 00:35:00.960
Can we do that?

00:35:00.960 --> 00:35:03.300
Are the Sunnis capable of doing that?

00:35:03.300 --> 00:35:10.110
Or do we need to take a neutral sort of Obama-like
position and say, well, we're talking to everybody

00:35:10.110 --> 00:35:11.120
but we're not committing?

00:35:11.120 --> 00:35:13.480
DAVIDSON: I think you've spoken to that a
little.

00:35:13.480 --> 00:35:21.300
So, David, why don't you-do you want to-
LESCH: I have taken the position, and in some

00:35:21.300 --> 00:35:26.870
places it's popular and in some places it
isn't, that the Obama administration as carried

00:35:26.870 --> 00:35:29.330
out the correct policy with regard to Syria.

00:35:29.330 --> 00:35:36.780
I tend to agree with what Josh has said, in
that trying to interpret and calibrate your

00:35:36.780 --> 00:35:45.040
actions in an area like the Middle East, especially
in what it has become, is just foolhardy,

00:35:45.040 --> 00:35:46.040
in my opinion.

00:35:46.040 --> 00:35:51.060
I mean, the mistakes that I think the Obama
administration has made has been in managing

00:35:51.060 --> 00:35:53.320
expectations around this.

00:35:53.320 --> 00:35:59.221
The biggest mistake, you know, in this-certainly
outside of Bashar al-Assad's initial mistake

00:35:59.221 --> 00:36:05.410
of cracking down harshly on the protestors-was
the West assuming that he was going to fall,

00:36:05.410 --> 00:36:09.350
and following, you know, what happened in
Egypt, and Tunisia, and Libya, and elsewhere.

00:36:09.350 --> 00:36:12.060
And of course, that didn't happen.

00:36:12.060 --> 00:36:18.850
And what that did, is by saying he's illegitimate,
by telling him to step aside or step down,

00:36:18.850 --> 00:36:24.250
it backed the regime in a corner where the
only choice was to fight in order to maintain

00:36:24.250 --> 00:36:25.250
power.

00:36:25.250 --> 00:36:30.600
In my mind, in meeting with Russians from
very early on, they had a better understanding

00:36:30.600 --> 00:36:36.660
of the situation in Syria very, very early
on, that Assad was going to stay in power

00:36:36.660 --> 00:36:42.490
and that this thing was going to be a protracted
conflict for a number of years.

00:36:42.490 --> 00:36:44.670
Q: Patrick Theros.

00:36:44.670 --> 00:36:46.350
I run the U.S.-Qatar Business Council.

00:36:46.350 --> 00:36:48.300
I served in Damascus in happier days.

00:36:48.300 --> 00:36:50.260
First of all, let me congratulate you.

00:36:50.260 --> 00:36:54.470
This is the most rational discussion group
on Syria that I've heard in the last year.

00:36:54.470 --> 00:36:55.470
So very good.

00:36:55.470 --> 00:36:56.470
(Laughter.)

00:36:56.470 --> 00:37:00.080
But I noticed that the word "Baath" almost
never appeared in your conversation.

00:37:00.080 --> 00:37:07.560
Are there any larger than sectarian institutions
left in the Levant, or have they all gone

00:37:07.560 --> 00:37:08.560
away?

00:37:08.560 --> 00:37:09.560
DAVIDSON: Andrew.

00:37:09.560 --> 00:37:16.040
TABLER: Well, yes, I mean, theoretically you
have not just the Ba'ath party still functioning,

00:37:16.040 --> 00:37:21.070
you have the Syrian state.

00:37:21.070 --> 00:37:23.120
What does it mean anymore?

00:37:23.120 --> 00:37:26.510
Theoretically it's there, at the core of the
regime.

00:37:26.510 --> 00:37:33.630
It gives a veneer or some sort of Pan-Arab
rallying ethos, or whatever you want to call

00:37:33.630 --> 00:37:36.180
it, theory.

00:37:36.180 --> 00:37:41.600
But the sectarianism, which was always right
below the surface in Syria, I think now is

00:37:41.600 --> 00:37:42.600
the primary dynamic.

00:37:42.600 --> 00:37:47.770
I mean, it gets talked-you know, when Josh
was talking about the Shia crescent.

00:37:47.770 --> 00:37:52.190
It used to be, when I lived in-first live
in the Middle East, that Sunni and Shia didn't

00:37:52.190 --> 00:37:53.910
come up as much.

00:37:53.910 --> 00:37:55.800
In Syria, it was always discouraged.

00:37:55.800 --> 00:37:58.520
Now, people talk about it a lot.

00:37:58.520 --> 00:38:04.100
Even businessmen from Damascus that I meet
when they come to Beirut speak in Sunni/Shia

00:38:04.100 --> 00:38:09.520
terms, which is interesting to me because
it's in a mixed area where these things traditionally

00:38:09.520 --> 00:38:11.030
didn't mean as much.

00:38:11.030 --> 00:38:14.730
DAVIDSON: The woman in the back.

00:38:14.730 --> 00:38:16.580
Q: Thank you.

00:38:16.580 --> 00:38:17.580
Hi.

00:38:17.580 --> 00:38:20.310
Barbara Slavin from the Atlantic Council.

00:38:20.310 --> 00:38:21.610
Great panel, I agree.

00:38:21.610 --> 00:38:27.940
One of you-I think perhaps it was David Lesch-said
that we thought that Bashar al-Assad was Fredo

00:38:27.940 --> 00:38:29.270
and he turned out to be Michael.

00:38:29.270 --> 00:38:30.270
(Laughter.)

00:38:30.270 --> 00:38:34.950
I think that's your line, so I hope I'm quoting
you correctly.

00:38:34.950 --> 00:38:39.880
Given everything that you've said, what's
the purpose of these peace talks that are

00:38:39.880 --> 00:38:42.520
going to resume, supposedly in Geneva?

00:38:42.520 --> 00:38:44.360
What's the best possible outcome?

00:38:44.360 --> 00:38:51.950
Is it just a continuation of a lesser level
of hostilities so that aid can be distributed?

00:38:51.950 --> 00:38:56.460
Do you have any expectation that it would
actually lead to a quote, unquote "settlement"?

00:38:56.460 --> 00:38:57.460
Thanks.

00:38:57.460 --> 00:38:59.160
LESCH: Yeah, good question.

00:38:59.160 --> 00:39:04.430
I have a good friend of mine who's the U.N.
liaison in Damascus.

00:39:04.430 --> 00:39:06.870
And a remarkable guy.

00:39:06.870 --> 00:39:11.070
I saw him in Beirut one of my trips to Beirut
last year.

00:39:11.070 --> 00:39:14.110
And I put to him kind of that same question.

00:39:14.110 --> 00:39:17.830
I said: You're putting your life in danger
and all of this, for what?

00:39:17.830 --> 00:39:20.200
Because it's just almost an impossible task.

00:39:20.200 --> 00:39:23.980
And, you know, very emotionally, he said:
We have to try.

00:39:23.980 --> 00:39:25.010
We have to try.

00:39:25.010 --> 00:39:26.310
We've got to do something.

00:39:26.310 --> 00:39:27.310
People are dying.

00:39:27.310 --> 00:39:28.850
The country is falling apart.

00:39:28.850 --> 00:39:34.030
And so you have these remarkable people that
are trying to do the impossible.

00:39:34.030 --> 00:39:40.280
And you know, they've given up these grandiose
attempts, in many ways.

00:39:40.280 --> 00:39:44.350
And some of the U.N. special envoys, you know,
they came in and they announced, you know,

00:39:44.350 --> 00:39:49.510
they wanted a holiday-you know, a three-day
holiday with Lakhdar Brahimi, and then Staffan

00:39:49.510 --> 00:39:50.890
de Mistura with the freeze and all that.

00:39:50.890 --> 00:39:54.640
These things never had a chance from the beginning,
but there's political pressure to do something

00:39:54.640 --> 00:39:55.640
dramatic.

00:39:55.640 --> 00:39:59.530
I think there's been a change with the U.N.
approach to this after a lot of people got

00:39:59.530 --> 00:40:03.110
to them, finally, and after the reality of
this situation that you have to try this incremental

00:40:03.110 --> 00:40:04.490
slow fix.

00:40:04.490 --> 00:40:08.350
And, you know, you have to work on the humanitarian
issues.

00:40:08.350 --> 00:40:12.840
Now, on the other side of that-and the U.N.
approach is the small steps hoping to build

00:40:12.840 --> 00:40:19.450
up confidence and trust and, you know, the
ink blot ceasefire type of approach.

00:40:19.450 --> 00:40:27.530
But now you have Russia and the United States
as well trying to congeal their regional allies

00:40:27.530 --> 00:40:29.010
behind a common approach.

00:40:29.010 --> 00:40:33.430
And that's been a positive thing, in my view.

00:40:33.430 --> 00:40:39.000
But you know, as long as Assad is there and
saying such things-and I'm not-Andrew and

00:40:39.000 --> 00:40:41.970
I were talking about this beforehand.

00:40:41.970 --> 00:40:43.910
Maybe you want to comment on it.

00:40:43.910 --> 00:40:51.970
You know, I'm not convinced that the Russians
are going to really put pressure on Assad

00:40:51.970 --> 00:40:57.540
to accede to a managed transition, even though
in track two talks that we've all been involved

00:40:57.540 --> 00:41:01.750
I with Russians, and speaking with Russian
officials, they all say-one of the first things

00:41:01.750 --> 00:41:04.170
out of their mouths is, you know, we don't
really like Assad.

00:41:04.170 --> 00:41:05.170
We're not committed to him.

00:41:05.170 --> 00:41:07.660
Yet, gee, they're putting in all these troops
and all that.

00:41:07.660 --> 00:41:10.260
They're committed to that-to him being a strategic
ally.

00:41:10.260 --> 00:41:16.240
They're committed to keeping this regime in
power, and it happens to be Assad there.

00:41:16.240 --> 00:41:23.450
And then what we were saying about Assad and
his view in terms of staying in power, I just-I

00:41:23.450 --> 00:41:29.050
just don't see something happening with regard
to these negotiations, particularly because

00:41:29.050 --> 00:41:35.900
the Kurds aren't in there, you know, which
is a major-(laughs)-a major issue in my view.

00:41:35.900 --> 00:41:39.690
And so it's just-I just don't think anything's
going to happen in the short term.

00:41:39.690 --> 00:41:42.070
But the key is to keep it-to keep it going.

00:41:42.070 --> 00:41:46.330
And that's what Staffan de Mistura is trying
to do-keep a process going so that it doesn't

00:41:46.330 --> 00:41:49.800
break down dramatically, as Geneva II did.

00:41:49.800 --> 00:41:58.260
LANDIS: You know, what the regime people say
is: We would go to any meeting that de Mistura

00:41:58.260 --> 00:42:01.450
invited us to, even to the wedding of his
son.

00:42:01.450 --> 00:42:03.020
(Laughter.)

00:42:03.020 --> 00:42:09.150
Then they say, well, what are we going to
negotiate with this delegation that's there?

00:42:09.150 --> 00:42:13.190
The only militia leader that's there is Mohammed
Alloush.

00:42:13.190 --> 00:42:16.420
And he owns Douma, or half of Douma.

00:42:16.420 --> 00:42:23.040
And we could negotiate to get that half of
Douma back from him, but none of them own

00:42:23.040 --> 00:42:24.040
anything.

00:42:24.040 --> 00:42:26.870
They don't own any territory in all of Syria.

00:42:26.870 --> 00:42:28.290
What are they going to negotiate with us?

00:42:28.290 --> 00:42:29.740
They want Assad to go.

00:42:29.740 --> 00:42:31.180
We're not going to give it to them.

00:42:31.180 --> 00:42:34.230
And that's the end of the discussion, because
they don't own any land.

00:42:34.230 --> 00:42:36.190
LESCH: Can I just add something to that?

00:42:36.190 --> 00:42:40.790
I talked with one of the top security guys
of Assad in Beirut.

00:42:40.790 --> 00:42:42.720
And it was after Geneva II.

00:42:42.720 --> 00:42:45.940
And he was the guy in the back row who was
really running things, not Bouthaina Shaaban,

00:42:45.940 --> 00:42:46.940
not Jaafari.

00:42:46.940 --> 00:42:49.850
You know, this guy is the one who was running
it.

00:42:49.850 --> 00:42:52.460
And he was bemoaning this Geneva II meeting.

00:42:52.460 --> 00:42:55.480
And he said exactly, you know, reflecting
what Josh said.

00:42:55.480 --> 00:42:59.540
He said, I can get on the phone and stop fighting
on our side on all the fronts.

00:42:59.540 --> 00:43:04.200
Now, I don't know if they could do that again,
because it's become kind of, you know, dispersed,

00:43:04.200 --> 00:43:05.370
the regime forces.

00:43:05.370 --> 00:43:07.869
But I could get on the phone and I could stop
it.

00:43:07.869 --> 00:43:13.250
Who on the-who on this delegation can call
and stop the fighting on the opposition side

00:43:13.250 --> 00:43:15.060
more than one little area, one little zone?

00:43:15.060 --> 00:43:16.060
So, you know, it's an issue.

00:43:16.060 --> 00:43:20.650
DAVIDSON: So, in a way, there's a power question
on the other side.

00:43:20.650 --> 00:43:22.760
LESCH: From the-
DAVIDSON: And then there's a-

00:43:22.760 --> 00:43:27.230
TABLER: Yeah, from the-but the Riyadh grouping
does represent a number of groups from throughout

00:43:27.230 --> 00:43:28.510
the country.

00:43:28.510 --> 00:43:33.810
But the problem with umbrella organizations
is that you're never really sure-you don't-you

00:43:33.810 --> 00:43:35.680
don't just have one address, right.

00:43:35.680 --> 00:43:37.010
And that is-that's a difficulty.

00:43:37.010 --> 00:43:42.200
And that's actually the U.S. homework in the-in
the negotiations, whereas the Russians have

00:43:42.200 --> 00:43:43.610
a different bit of homework.

00:43:43.610 --> 00:43:50.910
Theirs is to make less rigid a historically
very rigid regime.

00:43:50.910 --> 00:43:56.730
Q: Simon Henderson, the Washington Institute
for Near East Policy.

00:43:56.730 --> 00:43:59.590
Thank you very much.

00:43:59.590 --> 00:44:02.300
The title of this is "Power Profile."

00:44:02.300 --> 00:44:11.870
At the moment, I don't feel that I know Bashar
al-Assad any better than what I did when I

00:44:11.870 --> 00:44:12.870
walked in the room.

00:44:12.870 --> 00:44:21.300
And what are the adjectives that-or the single
adjectives that you would use to describe

00:44:21.300 --> 00:44:22.690
him?

00:44:22.690 --> 00:44:28.670
We've heard that he's influenced by the hard-liners,
the reformists, his wife.

00:44:28.670 --> 00:44:33.000
But-you probably haven't seen it, and I haven't
seen it-but there's presumably a personality

00:44:33.000 --> 00:44:35.650
profile of him written somewhere in the CIA.

00:44:35.650 --> 00:44:37.869
What does it say?

00:44:37.869 --> 00:44:40.020
To me he looks like a chinless geek.

00:44:40.020 --> 00:44:41.920
But is he-is he indecisive?

00:44:41.920 --> 00:44:44.200
Is he evil?

00:44:44.200 --> 00:44:46.490
Is he delusional?

00:44:46.490 --> 00:44:49.750
What are the words, the single words, you
would use to describe him?

00:44:49.750 --> 00:44:52.770
DAVIDSON: Which again, might be useful for
President Trump.

00:44:52.770 --> 00:44:53.910
(Laughter.)

00:44:53.910 --> 00:44:58.850
TABLER: So I think the appropriate term here-I
mean, and I've, you know, struggled with this

00:44:58.850 --> 00:45:00.900
over the years and when I lived in Syria.

00:45:00.900 --> 00:45:05.050
And I earlier used the word "moody."

00:45:05.050 --> 00:45:08.640
I think that's true.

00:45:08.640 --> 00:45:16.280
And-but it doesn't mean that he's not intelligent
certainly and able to maneuver.

00:45:16.280 --> 00:45:19.830
I think the term that I've settled on is "borderline
personality."

00:45:19.830 --> 00:45:24.780
I think it-he sometimes has bouts of where
he's extremely rational and you can deal with

00:45:24.780 --> 00:45:28.510
it and then other times when I, for the life
of me, can't figure out what he's doing.

00:45:28.510 --> 00:45:31.090
And I don't think the people around him can
either.

00:45:31.090 --> 00:45:32.620
DAVIDSON: All right.

00:45:32.620 --> 00:45:34.150
Quick, what words?

00:45:34.150 --> 00:45:38.650
LESCH: "Measured" and "desperate."

00:45:38.650 --> 00:45:47.150
Measured because he doesn't make decisions
decisively or dramatically and he likes to

00:45:47.150 --> 00:45:48.320
think things over.

00:45:48.320 --> 00:45:52.860
Whether that's an active or passive approach,
it depends on the situation.

00:45:52.860 --> 00:46:01.310
Desperate, I think, now because I think he
realizes that, you know, for one, this dream

00:46:01.310 --> 00:46:08.450
he had of Syria being, you know, this internationally
recognized country that's integrated into

00:46:08.450 --> 00:46:12.500
the world community just isn't going to happen.

00:46:12.500 --> 00:46:20.140
And he realizes that he's-he has to rely on
the Russians and the Iranians and Hezbollah

00:46:20.140 --> 00:46:21.760
to stay-to stay in power.

00:46:21.760 --> 00:46:24.310
So-
DAVIDSON: How about your words?

00:46:24.310 --> 00:46:26.470
LANDIS: Yeah, I think he's rational.

00:46:26.470 --> 00:46:29.550
He's-he is very limited by his world.

00:46:29.550 --> 00:46:31.060
He got out to Europe once.

00:46:31.060 --> 00:46:35.930
He's gotten a Syrian education, which isn't
a great education.

00:46:35.930 --> 00:46:43.970
And he's-the job was way too big for him.

00:46:43.970 --> 00:46:44.970
He's shy.

00:46:44.970 --> 00:46:47.450
He's a little bit indecisive.

00:46:47.450 --> 00:46:52.530
But he's allowed the people around him in
a sense to-he forms a consensus with the-with

00:46:52.530 --> 00:46:54.780
the major security guys around him.

00:46:54.780 --> 00:47:00.770
He's got a loyal crew that are working hard
and doing-but he's in a very brittle regime

00:47:00.770 --> 00:47:05.190
where he cannot give up power for the Alawites
because all the people around him will be

00:47:05.190 --> 00:47:07.490
killed very quickly.

00:47:07.490 --> 00:47:09.650
And I think that's the major constraint.

00:47:09.650 --> 00:47:11.740
He's trying to keep those people alive.

00:47:11.740 --> 00:47:16.070
And the entire Alawite community, vast majority,
agree with him.

00:47:16.070 --> 00:47:20.260
And you can argue that he's backed them into
it and he should have just flown away and

00:47:20.260 --> 00:47:28.660
they would have been OK, but that's-you know,
he did save his crew, and ultimately he dragged

00:47:28.660 --> 00:47:32.840
his entire, you know, nation into this conflagration.

00:47:32.840 --> 00:47:38.460
But he may come out of it with the people
of the coast alive.

00:47:38.460 --> 00:47:42.710
And if he does, then he will see himself as
being a victor.

00:47:42.710 --> 00:47:49.300
DAVIDSON: I think part of your question was
also about Assad as a family member.

00:47:49.300 --> 00:47:54.430
Who does he-who's-when he-who is his family
in his view?

00:47:54.430 --> 00:48:02.109
Who are the people at the table who are part
of the Assad family, who he listens to?

00:48:02.109 --> 00:48:03.520
Do we know?

00:48:03.520 --> 00:48:09.930
TABLER: Yeah, I mean, we have-you know, we
have his immediate family-and I brought this

00:48:09.930 --> 00:48:14.780
along; I didn't think we were going to use
it-but this is-this is on our institute's

00:48:14.780 --> 00:48:15.780
website.

00:48:15.780 --> 00:48:17.820
And this talks about the members of the Assad
regime.

00:48:17.820 --> 00:48:20.760
And here you have-the red lines are blood
relations.

00:48:20.760 --> 00:48:22.090
The white ones are non-blood.

00:48:22.090 --> 00:48:24.420
And then it's broken down by sect.

00:48:24.420 --> 00:48:25.740
So we know who's in the regime.

00:48:25.740 --> 00:48:30.940
I think the hardest part is the visibility
inside of the regime in terms of the relationships

00:48:30.940 --> 00:48:34.060
between these people at any one time is difficult
to ascertain.

00:48:34.060 --> 00:48:41.890
And therefore it gets down to if you decide
to pressure such a regime or take military

00:48:41.890 --> 00:48:45.130
action against such a regime, for example,
you don't really know exactly what's going

00:48:45.130 --> 00:48:46.450
to happen.

00:48:46.450 --> 00:48:47.450
We know who's there.

00:48:47.450 --> 00:48:50.030
We just don't know what they would do in certain
situations.

00:48:50.030 --> 00:48:55.760
LANDIS: And the family-you know, if we compare
it to the Saddam Hussein family, which, I

00:48:55.760 --> 00:49:01.109
think, is an apt comparison because both are-represent
small minorities that dominated their countries

00:49:01.109 --> 00:49:04.490
through a single party, Ba'ath Party, for
a long time.

00:49:04.490 --> 00:49:06.870
The Assad family is fairly functional.

00:49:06.870 --> 00:49:09.630
They've never killed anybody within the family.

00:49:09.630 --> 00:49:11.160
They've always been polite.

00:49:11.160 --> 00:49:19.650
Even Rifaat al-Assad, who did try a coup when
his brother had a heart attack in '84, gave-got

00:49:19.650 --> 00:49:23.460
three chances: sent out of the country, came
back in; sent out of the country; came back

00:49:23.460 --> 00:49:26.600
in, finally sent into exile with billions
of dollars.

00:49:26.600 --> 00:49:31.540
And-but nobody in the family has ever been
killed by another family member.

00:49:31.540 --> 00:49:37.050
And there is-we can argue about, you know,
the-before he was a family member, the brother-in-law-

00:49:37.050 --> 00:49:38.050
TABLER: (Inaudible.)

00:49:38.050 --> 00:49:39.050
(Laughter.)

00:49:39.050 --> 00:49:44.020
LANDIS: But once he became a-once he married
the sister, they were good to go, and he was

00:49:44.020 --> 00:49:45.020
made head of intelligence.

00:49:45.020 --> 00:49:46.020
He was in.

00:49:46.020 --> 00:49:50.510
So, you know, there's the sort of Mafiosi
paradigm.

00:49:50.510 --> 00:49:54.119
But the family has been-is a middle class
family.

00:49:54.119 --> 00:50:01.300
Hafez al-Assad was a dignitary who signed
petitions under the French, the father, Sulayman

00:50:01.300 --> 00:50:02.440
al-Assad.

00:50:02.440 --> 00:50:07.730
So there's three generations of being fairly
upper middle class Alawites, which is very

00:50:07.730 --> 00:50:11.130
poor, but they came from good stock.

00:50:11.130 --> 00:50:17.420
Saddam Hussein was an orphan who was taken
in by an uncle, who was a criminal, and he

00:50:17.420 --> 00:50:19.260
became a criminal himself.

00:50:19.260 --> 00:50:21.380
And he killed lots of his family members.

00:50:21.380 --> 00:50:29.680
So he didn't hold people together through
consensus and using the soft touch as much

00:50:29.680 --> 00:50:30.750
as the Assads did.

00:50:30.750 --> 00:50:35.540
Now, obviously once that falls apart, that
soft touch-Syria was a much softer-touch place

00:50:35.540 --> 00:50:40.250
than Iraq was, but now it's fallen apart and
it's becoming like Iraq.

00:50:40.250 --> 00:50:46.490
And-but the family has held together in large
part because it hasn't been a terribly dysfunctional

00:50:46.490 --> 00:50:47.490
family.

00:50:47.490 --> 00:50:53.150
It listens to the people around them and unlike
Saddam's family, which was extremely dysfunctional

00:50:53.150 --> 00:50:56.130
and crumbled quite quickly.

00:50:56.130 --> 00:51:00.240
DAVIDSON: Back there.

00:51:00.240 --> 00:51:02.990
Q: Hey.

00:51:02.990 --> 00:51:04.280
Mark Kimmitt.

00:51:04.280 --> 00:51:08.470
Back to the question of power profile, you
said that he had about a three-year tutelage

00:51:08.470 --> 00:51:13.030
from when Bassel died until he took over-maybe.

00:51:13.030 --> 00:51:21.460
But the issue that I've got is if he's being
tutored by Hassan Khalil, Assef Shawkat, Bushra,

00:51:21.460 --> 00:51:27.470
Maher, Rifaat, how could he then have declared
the Damascus Spring?

00:51:27.470 --> 00:51:29.350
That's not exactly a reformist group.

00:51:29.350 --> 00:51:35.840
TABLER: And he had served in Lebanon, you
know, as well in the armed forces.

00:51:35.840 --> 00:51:38.990
It's a good question.

00:51:38.990 --> 00:51:46.050
I've-I think the one-the one thing that struck
me early on in going to Syria-and I started

00:51:46.050 --> 00:51:54.240
going there in 2000, 2001, shortly after he
died-is that-was that he-somehow the expectations

00:51:54.240 --> 00:51:58.340
that David talked about that he was actually
a Westerner or had been westernized were completely

00:51:58.340 --> 00:51:59.340
false.

00:51:59.340 --> 00:52:01.820
I mean, he was just not-he did not spend a
lot of time there.

00:52:01.820 --> 00:52:05.510
Why he decided this, I think there are a couple
of theories.

00:52:05.510 --> 00:52:09.670
One is that he thought he was na�ve, which
we talked about earlier.

00:52:09.670 --> 00:52:14.710
There's also the-perhaps the model of the
Hundred Flowers Campaign, right.

00:52:14.710 --> 00:52:18.050
You let some people out of prison, you see
what they say, and you see who goes back in.

00:52:18.050 --> 00:52:20.859
And then you blame it on the old guard, which
they did.

00:52:20.859 --> 00:52:24.710
They blamed everything on the old guard, when
actually it was the security apparatus, which

00:52:24.710 --> 00:52:26.930
was probably closest to him.

00:52:26.930 --> 00:52:29.650
Beyond that, I don't know.

00:52:29.650 --> 00:52:33.980
LESCH: If I can add, I mean, a little, you
know, different theory-and again, we ultimately

00:52:33.980 --> 00:52:35.380
don't know what was in his head.

00:52:35.380 --> 00:52:43.720
I mean, I actually believe that he intended
to incrementally reform the system but he

00:52:43.720 --> 00:52:50.420
just ran into this, he inherited a dilapidated,
stultifying system, the so-called old guard;

00:52:50.420 --> 00:52:54.930
and realized that he just could not make the
changes he wanted.

00:52:54.930 --> 00:52:59.680
And he wasn't able to do so really until,
you know, five or six years in his tenure

00:52:59.680 --> 00:53:00.680
in power.

00:53:00.680 --> 00:53:05.900
And you know, I always have this image-and
some people in Damascus at the time-you know,

00:53:05.900 --> 00:53:09.380
they had this-they were telling me this, that-you
know, that with, you know, the Damascus Spring,

00:53:09.380 --> 00:53:14.640
which-you know, radio stations and private
newspapers and salons and so forth and prisoner

00:53:14.640 --> 00:53:20.380
releases-that, you know, some of the security
apparatus just came around to him and said,

00:53:20.380 --> 00:53:24.590
you know, hey, we-this is not how we do things;
this is going to cause a lot more problems.

00:53:24.590 --> 00:53:27.300
And therefore Damascus winter set in due to
that.

00:53:27.300 --> 00:53:33.230
And so he finally realized that he couldn't
do what he wanted to do.

00:53:33.230 --> 00:53:38.210
Q: John Negroponte.

00:53:38.210 --> 00:53:39.450
You have a very difficult task.

00:53:39.450 --> 00:53:46.280
I mean, having overseen leadership profiles
or briefed them to the president or to other

00:53:46.280 --> 00:53:49.690
leaders in our government, it's not an easy
thing to do.

00:53:49.690 --> 00:53:53.630
And you can get things really right, but you
can also get them terribly wrong.

00:53:53.630 --> 00:53:56.750
And-but obviously it's worth working at.

00:53:56.750 --> 00:54:05.890
I have a question about how the Israelis see
Bashar, because I-my impression over the years

00:54:05.890 --> 00:54:10.420
has been that their impression of the Syrian
leadership is more benign, they see it as

00:54:10.420 --> 00:54:12.230
in their interest that he stay in power.

00:54:12.230 --> 00:54:14.750
I don't know if that's still their attitude.

00:54:14.750 --> 00:54:15.750
But how do they view him?

00:54:15.750 --> 00:54:16.890
DAVIDSON: Great question.

00:54:16.890 --> 00:54:21.530
LESCH: I was-pursued that very question in
Israel.

00:54:21.530 --> 00:54:29.400
And I-you know, I met with-on one occasion-this
was before the uprising-you know, the chief

00:54:29.400 --> 00:54:35.820
of staff of the Israeli military at the time,
who thought that he was weak, which was-tended

00:54:35.820 --> 00:54:41.540
to be a dominant view when he came to power,
that he was weak and incompetent, was Fredo,

00:54:41.540 --> 00:54:42.590
you know.

00:54:42.590 --> 00:54:47.350
And then I met with his deputy, who thought,
you know, very much differently, that he had

00:54:47.350 --> 00:54:51.740
some promise, that, you know, he could keep
this thing together.

00:54:51.740 --> 00:54:55.760
And of course as the uprising came in, there's
been this divide, you know, in Israel to a

00:54:55.760 --> 00:55:02.640
certain degree, you know, regarding, you know,
is it best to see him go and therefore Iran

00:55:02.640 --> 00:55:09.900
and Hezbollah are weakened or, you know, he's
a devil-we-know type thing and we want stability.

00:55:09.900 --> 00:55:17.790
I had one Israeli top general tell me, you
know, during-a couple of years ago in answer

00:55:17.790 --> 00:55:22.330
to that question, and he basically said-and
this was recently repeated by another Israeli

00:55:22.330 --> 00:55:28.630
military leader, I think, last week or something-that
we'd rather have ISIS on our border than Iran

00:55:28.630 --> 00:55:33.610
and-because we can-because, this general told
me, we can deal with those guys.

00:55:33.610 --> 00:55:36.500
Iran is a much-is a much bigger problem from
their perspective.

00:55:36.500 --> 00:55:45.080
TABLER: My last conversations with-in Israel
is that they have been confused.

00:55:45.080 --> 00:55:50.550
It was-they used the Menachem Begin line from
the Iran-Iraq War: We wish both sides well.

00:55:50.550 --> 00:55:51.810
(Laughter.)

00:55:51.810 --> 00:55:53.070
And I think that's true.

00:55:53.070 --> 00:55:59.530
The-but the problem-and David said it right-you
know, ISIS and jihadist groups for them are

00:55:59.530 --> 00:56:03.010
a tactical threat, not a strategic threat.

00:56:03.010 --> 00:56:09.790
The problem is the reliable manpower pools
that Assad has had to rely on are organized

00:56:09.790 --> 00:56:11.490
largely by the Iranians on the ground.

00:56:11.490 --> 00:56:13.570
It's not only, and they're not only Iranian.

00:56:13.570 --> 00:56:14.570
Don't get me wrong.

00:56:14.570 --> 00:56:19.270
But they don't like the fact that Hezbollah
and these Shia militia are showing up in Shaykh

00:56:19.270 --> 00:56:20.690
Maskin in the south.

00:56:20.690 --> 00:56:25.109
That's in their sphere of influence, and they
were-they say they were promised by Putin

00:56:25.109 --> 00:56:30.780
when Bibi went to meet him after the-shortly
after the intervention that the Russians would

00:56:30.780 --> 00:56:34.880
not support such campaigns, because the Russians
are in southern Syria doing turns over the

00:56:34.880 --> 00:56:36.490
Golan Heights, right.

00:56:36.490 --> 00:56:40.430
So in exchange for that, you know, looking
the other way, they weren't supposed to support

00:56:40.430 --> 00:56:41.430
Iranian forces.

00:56:41.430 --> 00:56:43.160
But then they did.

00:56:43.160 --> 00:56:45.850
And this is a big concern for Israel going
forward.

00:56:45.850 --> 00:56:48.200
And I-and I-and I honestly don't know how
they're going to deal with it.

00:56:48.200 --> 00:56:50.250
I would expect with more strikes.

00:56:50.250 --> 00:56:51.250
DAVIDSON: All right.

00:56:51.250 --> 00:56:53.460
We've only got a couple of minutes left.

00:56:53.460 --> 00:56:55.480
So quick question from there.

00:56:55.480 --> 00:56:59.490
Q: Hi, Jeff Smith, the Center for Public Integrity.

00:56:59.490 --> 00:57:03.859
The overall impression I get from the conversation
so far, which has been excellent, is of a

00:57:03.859 --> 00:57:06.830
lot of players who are stuck.

00:57:06.830 --> 00:57:08.369
We're stuck.

00:57:08.369 --> 00:57:09.849
Assad is stuck.

00:57:09.849 --> 00:57:11.580
The Russians are stuck.

00:57:11.580 --> 00:57:16.120
And so in a very unfair way, could you please
open your crystal ball and tell us what you

00:57:16.120 --> 00:57:20.310
think will be present five years from now
in Damascus?

00:57:20.310 --> 00:57:21.310
DAVIDSON: That's a great question.

00:57:21.310 --> 00:57:22.310
What's the-
Q: I'd like you to just look forward.

00:57:22.310 --> 00:57:26.119
You've given us a really good sense of where
things are right now, but project forward

00:57:26.119 --> 00:57:28.390
from where we are right now.

00:57:28.390 --> 00:57:29.530
This is an unfair question.

00:57:29.530 --> 00:57:33.080
I know it's difficult, but I'd still like
to hear what you have to say.

00:57:33.080 --> 00:57:34.080
DAVIDSON: And we've got two minutes.

00:57:34.080 --> 00:57:37.210
LANDIS: If I could start, I'll give-I'll just
complete the little painting I started out

00:57:37.210 --> 00:57:38.210
with.

00:57:38.210 --> 00:57:42.080
If the United States and the international
community had been willing to spend real money

00:57:42.080 --> 00:57:50.950
in Syria, one would have done a Yugoslavia
and created a Sunni state where ISIS conquered

00:57:50.950 --> 00:57:52.590
easily.

00:57:52.590 --> 00:57:57.380
We're not going to do that because we've stuck
to our international borders and our plan

00:57:57.380 --> 00:58:03.410
of power sharing amongst all these different
sects and returning Iraq and Syria to there.

00:58:03.410 --> 00:58:08.570
And that means that we bowed out really.

00:58:08.570 --> 00:58:13.900
America is busy destroying the Sunnis of Iraq
with a very sectarian Shiite regime.

00:58:13.900 --> 00:58:18.720
Russia is busy destroying the Sunnis of Syria
with a very Shiite sectarian regime.

00:58:18.720 --> 00:58:26.270
And we're both cooperating, in a sense, to
destroy the remaining Sunni powers that remain

00:58:26.270 --> 00:58:27.270
in there.

00:58:27.270 --> 00:58:28.750
They will be oppressed.

00:58:28.750 --> 00:58:31.490
It'll be unstable.

00:58:31.490 --> 00:58:35.640
Assad will win, I think, a large hunk of Syria,
if not all of it, back over the next three

00:58:35.640 --> 00:58:39.550
or four years, and-with Russian help and Iranian
help.

00:58:39.550 --> 00:58:44.390
And there will be a Shiite crescent that will
probably go for the next 20 years or something

00:58:44.390 --> 00:58:50.600
defining that security zone, and America is
going to increasingly be anxious about a(n)

00:58:50.600 --> 00:58:54.240
unstable Gulf with low oil prices.

00:58:54.240 --> 00:58:57.840
And that's where I see our-I see a lot of
instability.

00:58:57.840 --> 00:59:01.119
I see us in the middle of this great sorting
out.

00:59:01.119 --> 00:59:06.160
And I think we're going to see great instability
and oppression in the Middle East for the

00:59:06.160 --> 00:59:07.290
decades to come.

00:59:07.290 --> 00:59:10.460
DAVIDSON: Wow, that is-
LANDIS: My bright picture.

00:59:10.460 --> 00:59:11.460
(Laughter.)

00:59:11.460 --> 00:59:13.250
LESCH: On that note, right?

00:59:13.250 --> 00:59:14.250
(Laughter.)

00:59:14.250 --> 00:59:19.680
DAVIDSON: Perhaps that's where-that's where
we need to end, since we're at the hour.

00:59:19.680 --> 00:59:21.730
If you have a quick-
TABLER: I only have one last thing.

00:59:21.730 --> 00:59:30.390
So a colleague mine saw Assad a few days ago,
and one of the big takeaways from his meeting

00:59:30.390 --> 00:59:38.420
was that Assad said openly in the meeting
that he was very eager to get along-get a

00:59:38.420 --> 00:59:44.660
process going because-before the general election
here in the United States, which, of course,

00:59:44.660 --> 00:59:49.090
if you read President Obama's interview in
The Atlantic, it wouldn't be a surprise why,

00:59:49.090 --> 00:59:50.090
right?

00:59:50.090 --> 00:59:53.230
I mean, Syria has become, unfortunately, a
very controversial issue.

00:59:53.230 --> 00:59:55.040
Barack Obama's very staked out on this.

00:59:55.040 --> 01:00:00.560
The presidential candidates are not as staked
out, but, you know, certainly Hillary Clinton

01:00:00.560 --> 01:00:05.440
has a different-has a slightly different view,
I think, than Obama, but hardly anything clear

01:00:05.440 --> 01:00:09.880
that would-that would lead us to a direct
military intervention or anything like that.

01:00:09.880 --> 01:00:11.460
DAVIDSON: That's a fascinating note to end
on.

01:00:11.460 --> 01:00:17.859
And I'm sorry we didn't get to talk more about
the American election and so many other things.

